Documentation for IFU workflow
==============================

*v. 20230202*

This document details the workflow shown in the relevant html file
included within this repository. Please use this guide in conjunction
with the html file. Hyperlinks therein (depicted as globes) provide
access to the resources described here.

Bitbucket does not render the html in-page. You can use the following link
to quickly access to a mirror (which eventually could *not* be synched with
the latest version of any branch of this repository):

http://casu.ast.cam.ac.uk/~dmurphy/weave/ifu_workflow/ifu_workflow.html

**Installation:** please refer to the TUTORIAL.md file for installation
instuctions.

**Please note:** during development, not all resources are available, and
links will be generated when sample files, code is committed.

**Disclaimer:** this software is designed to aide in the preparation of
WEAVE IFU observations. We provide no warranty for this software, and
offer support on a best-effort basis.

**Definitions:**

* CNAME: an identifier that maps to sky position. Used to identify WEAVE targets.
* End-user: the user that is preparing IFU observations
* OR: the WEAVE Operational Repository - http://casu.ast.cam.ac.uk/weave/
* SPA-columns: the mandatory WEAVE SPA columns that must be included in all input FITS catalogues
* WASP: the WEAVE Automated Submission Platform - http://wasp.ast.cam.ac.uk/

Stage 0: Target selection
-------------------------

The end-user must compile an initial list of IFU
targets. Consideration must be made of the instrument mode the
observations are made in, what dither strategy is to be used, what
targets are to be provided.

We suggest defining the survey itself, and the planned targets in a CSV
file - an example pair of CSVs can be found in workflow/stage1/input. Users
can copy these and adapt to their own needs.

Please read the comments in these 2 CSV files to usage instructions. Users
need to supply their TACALLOC and TAC_ID codes. These can be found in the
WASP under the "My Survey" page.

Stage 1: Creation of IFU driver catalogue
-----------------------------------------

**Contributors:** David Murphy, Luis Peralta

The information from stage 0 needs to be standardised into a
preliminary FITS catalogue containing "IFU drivers": IFU target
positions in each row alongside other critical columns that need to be
propagated through into the XML-based OB files. The example data in
stage1 provides a template for what quantities must be included, but
should ideally encompass specific SPA-columns required for assembling
the XML files.

Stage 2: Creation of the XML files with the targets
---------------------------------------------------

**Contributors:** David Murphy, Luis Peralta

The code at stage 2 analyses the input IFU driver catalogue and
converts this list into a series of distinct pointings (grouping mIFU
pointings into fields where required). This process outputs the
pointings as XML files (using the base XML template), filling the
details as interpreted from the PROGTEMP and OBSTEMP codes. For
instances where users provide more candidate bundles than can be
accommodated per WEAVE mIFU field (generally 18 or 19), then the
bundles are distributed depending on one of three options set by
the user.


Stage 3: Adding guide and calibration stars to the XML files
------------------------------------------------------------

**Contributors:** David Murphy, Luis Peralta

Each XML file is further developed by injecting the non-science target
information. This includes addition of the guidestar(s) for the LIFU
(mIFU) as well as calibration target options (more than one, to allow
for flexibility when allocating mIFU bundles). The selection of a
guidestar for LIFU also sets the required position angle (PA) at this
point. Users had the option to specify a requested PA at stage 1 and
determine if this is permitted based on availability of a suitable
guiding candidate. For LIFU pointings with specified preset dither
patterns (LIFU_1.xml in the stage3 example data), we require that the
selected guidestar be visible in each dither. In principle for a custom
dither pattern (LIFU_2.xml) end-users are able to specify a guide star
for each pointing. We recommend LIFU xmls use the same guide star for
each exposure. The output of this stage are XML files ready to be passed
to Configure.

Stage 4: Configuring the XML files
----------------------------------

**Contributors:** David Terrett

End-users are required to have a copy of Configure tools installed and
running for this stage. XML files are passed to the Configure
tools. In LIFU and mIFU cases, the files generated at Stage 2 have
associated .epoch and .ha files - these can be provided (as pipe inputs)
to Configure for providing it information on the epoch and desired hour
angle of observation.

For LIFU observations, Configure serves solely to populate the XML
with the LIFU array, including correct positions for the fibres. For
the mIFU, Configure involves a more interactive method of dropping mIFU
bundles onto input targets. The location of the sky mIFU bundles is
chosen at this stage. Configure then calculates the bundle rotation
and consequently fibre positions. For the mIFU, this can be an
iterative process, whereby un-allocated targets may be re-submitted
to Configure in order to generate a new XML file (and hence
observation) with these additional targets. For both IFU modes, where
required Configure will implement the pre-set dither pattern. This is
controlled by the apply_dither' attribute in the <dithering> element,
which in turn is inherited from the ifu_dither column in stage 0
catalogue.

Stage 5: Creation the IFU FITS catalogue
----------------------------------------

**Contributors:** David Murphy, Luis Peralta

The output of configure (previous stage) provides spatial data for all
fibres, at all dither positions. The workflow now takes these data and
converts each fibre into a row of a FITS file defined by the input
FITS catalogue template for that survey. This template will include
(as-yet empty) survey-specific columns and photometric data columns
to be added later.

Stage 6: Improving the IFU FITS catalogue (optional, but recommended)
---------------------------------------------------------------------

**Contributors:** Luis Peralta, Yago Ascasibar, Scott Trager

This code collection takes the stage 5 FITS catalogue and populates
all relevant missing columns. These fall into 3 categories:

* Photometric data: MAG_*, MAG_*_ERR, GAIA_MAG_* and GAIA_MAG_*_ERR.
  Using valid input imaging, this module calculates the expected
  magnitude through the fibre.

* Additional fibre information: TARGUSE, TARGCLASS and APS_*.
  Linked to the previous module, users may set specific priors on
  specific fibres. Examples include designating additional fibres
  within the LIFU array as sky (TARGUSE=S), assigning TARGCLASS
  values to fibres, and adjusting the APS analysis parameters for
  sources observed with particular fibres.

* The inclusion of survey-specific ancillary data. We provide a
  framework code here that end-users will need to adapt to include
  their additional data.

Stage 7: Creation of the combo FITS catalogue
---------------------------------------------

**Contributors:** David Murphy, Luis Peralta

The output of Stage 6 can now be merged with the equivalent MOS-based
catalogue. In any given submission round, each survey must submit just
one FITS file to the WASP for validation. This stage therefore concatenates
the MOS and IFU-based targets. This requires that the MOS
catalogue contains (as per template specification) the same columns,
and that these are populated and ready for WASP validation. WASP will
allow advance validation of these two components via the test channel,
to ensure that (for example) the MOS targets are legal prior to the
merge.

This stage should still be run, even if there is no MOS catalogue to merge
with (in which case the catalogue can be left as None). This is because this
stage also filters out calibration and sky mIFU bundles that do not need to
be stored in the FITS catalogue (unless special processing requests have been
specified for them).

Stage 8: Submission to WASP of the combo FITS catalogue
-------------------------------------------------------

**Contributors:** David Murphy, Clare Worley

The combo FITS file is submitted to WASP for validation.
After WASP has validated the combined FITS catalogue, a copy of the
validated file is available to download from the WASP. This is the
version that has CASU-generated CNAMES. The WEAVE Survey Working Group
(SWG) takes these files to prepare MOS Observing Blocks.

Stage 9: Finishing the XML files
--------------------------------

**Contributors:** Luis Peralta, Sarah Hughes, David Murphy

End-users for the IFU workflow also take the WASP-validated FITS
catalogue and generate XML OB files. The code collection in stage 9
takes the output from the WASP validation and combines it with the
**input** to stage 5 (ie, the output of Configure) to generate
WEAVE-ready OBs. These will include all relevant quantities specified
at stage 6 in addition to CNAMES from the FITS catalogue.

It is known that this process can be time-consuming where there are a
large number of FITS rows (often due to a large number of OBs and dither
pointings). A multi-processing option is provided to improve processing
times.

Once these candidate OBs have been generated, some may require linking
such that the scheduler can connect observation of one field with the
observation of another distinct field. We demonstrate this here by
providing an example linking CSV file. This allows the user to define
group linkages between the OBs (via linked_group or obsgroup) subject to
some constraints and limitations. Please check the stage9 README and the
example stage9 input file for information and examples of this process.

Stage 10: Submission to WASP of the XML files
---------------------------------------------

**Contributors:** David Murphy, Clare Worley

Once the XML files in stage 9 have been generated, end-users must
wait until WASP opens for OB submissions for this round. Users will
receive an email notification when this window opens, but validation
of these files ahead of this window can be checked via the test
channel open to all WASP users. Bulk submission of these files can be
done by generating a tarball and submitting this to the WASP, as per
instructions. For OB submissions, WASP permits incremental uploads of
(for example) MOS-only fields followed by IFU-only fields.

Stage 11: Happy ending
----------------------

Once your XML IFU submissions have been successfully validated, the
WASP adds CNAMES to any sky fibres that do not have CNAME data. Your
targets are now prepared for observations at WHT - congratulations!
You can check the progress of your observations at the WEAVE
Operational Repository (OR).

